---
title: Instruments and sound banks
description: Tune and loop an instrument in the Sound editor, and add instruments for songs to a sound bank of your own.
sidebar:
  order: 3
---

A **sound bank** is a folder in `audio/bank/`, such as `audio/bank/SYS2/`, that holds up to 16 instruments. Each instrument is a mono WAV sample plus its tuning, an optional loop, and an envelope that shapes its volume. The bank's `sound_bank.xml` file lists its instruments and envelopes. Sound effects, ambient sounds, and songs play samples through the instruments in banks.

**New > Sound Effect** adds its sample to one of your mod's sound effect banks, `SFX1` to `SFX9`, and creates the bank if it needs to. See [Add a sound effect](/papermario-dx/audio/sound-effects/). You only make a bank by hand for instruments that songs play.

## Editing an instrument

Open a sample's WAV file in a bank's folder to edit it in the Sound editor. For a sample that loops, opening either of its WAV files shows the whole sample.

- **Root note** and the cents next to it are the pitch at which the sample plays unchanged. A one-shot sound effect plays note C3, so in a one-shot, a sample with a root note of C3 plays at its recorded pitch.
- **Envelope** chooses one of the bank's envelopes, which shape the volume while a note is held and after it's released.
- **Loop** is **off**, **forever**, or **a number of times**. See [Loop a sample](#loop-a-sample).
- Select **Play**, or a key on the on-screen keyboard, to hear the instrument.

To change part of the sample, select it on the waveform and right-click the selection. The menu has **Crop to Selection**, **Fade In**, **Fade Out**, **Normalize**, **Louder (+3 dB)**, and **Quieter (−3 dB)**. If the sample's rate is higher than 32 kHz, the rate the game plays sound at, select **Convert to 32 kHz**.

To replace a sample, drag a WAV file with the same name from your file manager onto the bank's folder in the Filesystem. The file must be mono, 16-bit PCM.

## Loop a sample

{% steps %}
1. Open the sample in the Sound editor.
2. Set **Loop** to **forever**, or to **a number of times** and enter how many times the loop plays.
3. Drag the loop marker to where the loop starts, or select the part of the waveform to loop from, right-click it, and select **Loop From Here**. The loop runs from there to the end of the sample.
{% /steps %}

In the bank's folder, a looping instrument has two WAV files: one for the part before the loop and one for the loop. The instrument's `loop` attribute in `sound_bank.xml` names the loop's file. The Sound editor names a new loop's file after the sample, such as `hum_loop.wav` for `hum.wav`.

## Adding an instrument for songs

A song can play instruments from banks that it loads, so its samples don't have to be in a bank that's always loaded. Give these samples a bank of their own. Don't add them to an `SFX` bank, because a bank that a song loads isn't always loaded, and sound effects can't play from it.

{% steps %}
1. In the Filesystem, right-click `audio/bank` and select **New > Folder**. Name the folder after the bank, with four characters at most, such as `MUS1`.
2. Drag your WAV file from your file manager onto the folder. The file must be mono, 16-bit PCM, and named in snake case, such as `chime.wav`. No other bank can have a sample with the same name.
3. Copy `sound_bank.xml` from another bank, paste it into your folder, and replace its contents with this:

    ```xml
    <?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
    <SoundBank>
        <Instruments>
            <Instrument wav="chime" envName="env1" keyBase="4800"/>
        </Instruments>
        <Envelopes>
            <Envelope envName="env1">
                <Commands>
                    <Press>
                        <Point duration="0" volume="7F"/>
                        <End/>
                    </Press>
                    <Release>
                        <Point duration="120ms" volume="00"/>
                        <End/>
                    </Release>
                </Commands>
            </Envelope>
        </Envelopes>
    </SoundBank>
    ```

4. Open `chime.wav` and set its root note and loop in the Sound editor.
5. Select **Game > Play**, so that the Song Editor lists the new instrument. Then [choose it for your song](/papermario-dx/audio/songs/#loading-extra-sound-banks-with-a-song).
{% /steps %}

To add more samples to the bank, add an `Instrument` element for each one at the end of `Instruments`. Songs refer to an instrument by its bank and its position in the bank, not by its name, so don't reorder or remove instruments that a song uses.

## Adding an envelope

The Sound editor's **Envelope** list shows the envelopes in the bank's `sound_bank.xml`. To add one, add an `Envelope` element to `Envelopes` with a new `envName`. `env1` in the example above holds the volume at full (`7F`) while a note is held, and fades it to silence in 120 milliseconds after the note is released. The game's banks, such as `audio/bank/SYS2/sound_bank.xml`, have more envelopes to copy.

## Limits

- A bank holds 16 instruments at most.
- A sample name can appear in only one bank.
- The game loads at most 79 always-loaded banks.
- A bank that a song loads can hold fewer instruments and envelopes than an always-loaded bank.
- Your audio must fit in the space the game can address. If it doesn't, use shorter samples or lower sample rates.
